1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: Today the same bookkeeping is drawn as a square, which Reginald Punnett devised decades later.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: Which means two round seeds in three should be hybrids, still hiding a small r. And Mendel tested that.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: You grow the seed, and look at the seeds its plant makes.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: That's one point nine three to one, close to the two to one he predicted.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: Next, Mendel crossed plants that differed in two traits, round yellow seeds with wrinkled green ones.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: The hybrids were all round and yellow. From fifteen of them, he harvested five hundred and fifty-six seeds, in four kinds.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: And two of those kinds are new. Round green and wrinkled yellow don't look like either original parent.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: That makes nine sixteenths round and yellow, and the four kinds come in nine, three, three and one.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: And he got three hundred and fifteen, and thirty-two. That's close.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: With three traits, he found twenty-seven kinds, and he bred all one hundred and twenty-eight pure combinations of the seven.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: About forty people listened, and his paper came out in the society's journal in eighteen sixty-six.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: Not total silence. But it was read as a paper on hybrids, and cited only about three times in thirty-five years.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: His hawkweed crosses didn't fit, since many hawkweeds, it turned out, make most of their seeds without fertilisation.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: In the spring of nineteen hundred, three plant scientists in three countries published within about two months, all pointing back to Mendel.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: Three at once, after thirty-four years. Was that a coincidence?

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: Historians now doubt that Chermak understood Mendel's laws, and how much de Vries owed to Mendel is still debated.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: Three to one is an average, though. Plant by plant, Mendel's counts wandered.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: One gave nineteen to ten, under two to one. And another, he noted, gave forty-three to two.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: Small counts swing widely, because chance decides which pollen meets which egg.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: And they scatter just like his, from twenty-one to eleven on one plant, to twenty-nine to five on another.
